[{:seon.task/name "a-long-string-does-not-starve-its-siblings"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-10-21 by session AGENTS: an eval item {:form \"(code-of 0)\" :out <9,600 chars of pprint output> :value (vec (range 2000))} printed under 545 tokens (seon.print/print-value, the last-evals share) shows 1,584 characters of :out and :value as `[…2000 (get-in (nth (my.turn/evals) 0) [:value])]`: not one element of the value the agent asked for. Cause: seon.print.select ranks a long string before a collection (member-rank, select.clj:109-118) and then the string's characters are allotted like one member, greedily, while the collection's members are a level deeper, so breadth-first never reaches them. Correct: a long string's characters are allotted in the same rounds as its siblings' members (a string is a sequence of lines for allotment), so every sibling shows its first part before any one deepens. A general printer improvement: every value with a long string beside a collection benefits (contract errors, test expected/actual, the context). Replaces nothing; the law tests in seon.print-test stay green."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.print-test/a-long-string-shares-the-budget-with-its-siblings}}]
